# skill-split-code-security.jsonl — ROADMAP 12's replacement trigger.
#
# SELECTION RULE — MEASUREMENT set, not a regression set. All twelve cases were written
# blind on 2026-09-09, BEFORE any model was called, from the two JOBS that
# `sota-code-security`'s rules files do: rules/01–09 name vulnerability CLASSES, and
# rules/10–15 verify that a CONTROL actually does something. Six of each, paired so the
# two halves cover comparable surfaces (auth, CI, parsers, crypto, logging, uploads).
# No case was selected, reworded, dropped or re-labelled on the basis of any model's
# answer — selection by outcome would guarantee the result and is the defect
# `sota-llm-engineering` rules/01 §8 describes.
#
# THE QUESTION: one `description` is the entire auto-load classifier for both jobs.
# ROADMAP 12 parked a skill-level split because a file COUNT was never a reason; the
# reason, if there is one, is that the combined description mis-classifies
# verification-shaped tasks. This set measures exactly that and nothing else.
#
# `distractor` is the sibling that shares surface vocabulary — for the verification
# half these are the skills a reader might plausibly reach for when the question is
# "does this control do anything?", which is precisely the confusion under test.
#
# Run: python3 evals/run-desc-routing.py --samples 3 --temp 0.7 \
#        --cases evals/cases/skill-split-code-security.jsonl
#
# --- vulnerability-class half (the job rules/01–09 do) ---
{"id": "v1_login_sqli", "task": "Our login handler builds its query by concatenating the submitted email into a SQL string. Review the handler and tell me what an attacker can do with it and how to fix it.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-databases"}
{"id": "v2_jwt_verify", "task": "Review how this service validates JWTs from our identity provider: it decodes the token, reads the claims, and trusts the `alg` header. What is wrong and what should it do instead?", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-identity-access"}
{"id": "v3_upload_path", "task": "Users upload avatars and we save them under a directory derived from the client-supplied filename. Find the problems with this and tell me how to accept uploads safely.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-api-design"}
{"id": "v4_pickle_cache", "task": "This worker deserializes cached job payloads with pickle. The cache is populated by another internal service. Is that a problem, and what would you change?", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-python"}
{"id": "v5_error_leak", "task": "Our 500 handler returns the exception message and stack trace to the caller, and we log the full request body including headers. Review both for what they expose.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-observability"}
{"id": "v6_feed_ingest", "task": "We pull third-party RSS feeds and render the item bodies in our web UI. Walk through what an attacker who controls a feed can do to us and how to ingest it safely.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-data-engineering"}
# --- control-verification half (the job rules/10–15 do) ---
{"id": "c1_inert_gate", "task": "Our CI has a step that greps the diff for hardcoded secrets and it has never once failed. I want to know whether it is actually checking anything or has been silently passing over an empty file list.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-devsecops"}
{"id": "c2_authz_noop", "task": "There is a permission decorator on every admin route. I want to establish whether it actually denies anything at runtime, or whether it would look identical if it were a no-op.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-testing"}
{"id": "c3_empty_comparand", "task": "A script compares the current vulnerability list against a baseline file and reports new findings. The baseline is generated by an earlier stage. Work out under what conditions this reports 'no new findings' while checking nothing.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-shell-scripting"}
{"id": "c4_rate_limit_effect", "task": "We have a rate limiter configured on the login endpoint. Rather than reading its config again, I want to know what observable difference there would be if it were doing nothing, and how to establish which of those two worlds we are in.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-performance"}
{"id": "c5_audit_ledger", "task": "A shell script writes our tamper-evident deployment audit trail. I need to assess whether the evidence it produces could be forged or silently dropped without anyone noticing.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-shell-scripting"}
{"id": "c6_coverage_census", "task": "The team says input validation is applied on every endpoint. I want to count how many endpoints the validation actually guards versus how many exist, and find the ones the claim skips.", "expect": "sota-code-security", "distractor": "sota-api-design"}
